==English==
{{was wotd|2024|May|15}}

===Etymology===
From {{compound|en|child|'s|play|notext=1|type=exocentric}}, originally referring literally to play by a child.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=6081665769|date=July 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=phrase|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220517064447/https://www.lexico.com/definition/child's_play}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈt͡ʃaɪldz ˌpleɪ/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-child's play.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} Something [[particularly]] [[easy#Adjective|easy]] or [[simple#Adjective|simple]].
#: {{synonyms|en|kid stuff|piece of cake}}
#: {{ux|en|Compared to my last job, this is '''child’s play'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1949|month=January and February|author=F. G. Roe|title=I Saw Three Englands–1|journal=Railway Magazine|page=11|text=I knew something of the railway engineer's uncanny genius for finding a path through such barriers if any path existed; yet I also knew the path would be no '''child's play'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=xp8IBgAAQBAJ&pg=PA51|title=The Road Not Taken and Other Poems: (Penguin Classics Deluxe Edition)|author=Robert Frost|publisher=Penguin|series=Penguin Classics Deluxe Edition|isbn=9780698141780|oclc=1155952570|year=1914|page=51|passage=And then he'd crow as if he thought that '''child's play''' — The only fun he had. I've heard them say, though, They found a way to put a stop to it. He was before my time—I never saw him; But the pen stayed exactly as it was There in the upper chamber in the ell, A sort of catch-all full of attic clutter.{{...}}}}

